IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `types`
IMPORT `chapter-4-exceptions`
IMPORT `chapter-4a-private-defence`

§ `Chapter 16 -- Offences affecting life`

-- Penal Code 1871 ss 299, 300, 301, 304A, 304B, 304C, 305, 306, 307, 308, 308A,
-- 308B, 310.
-- Source: registers/source-bundle/PC1871.txt.
--
-- Shape of the group. s 299 defines culpable homicide. s 300 makes culpable homicide
-- murder on any of four limbs, "except in the cases hereinafter excepted" -- seven
-- Exceptions, each of which returns the case to culpable homicide not amounting to
-- murder. So murder is a conjunction: culpable homicide, plus a s 300 limb, minus
-- every Exception. Everything else in the group is either a lesser homicide
-- (ss 304A, 310), a derived liability (ss 304B, 304C, 308A), an inchoate offence
-- (ss 305 to 308) or an offence against the body after death (s 308B).
--
-- PUNISHMENT IS NOT COMPUTED. ss 302, 304 and 311 are punishment sections and are
-- not encoded; "culpable homicide not amounting to murder" is decided here because
-- it is a classification, not a sentence. Where a section's only variation is in the
-- punishment -- s 305(1)(a) to (c), s 307(1) and (2), s 304C(4) -- the distinguishing
-- facts are carried so a caller can see them, but no term is computed.

§§ `Section 299 -- Culpable homicide`

-- Whoever causes death by doing an act with the intention of causing death, or with
-- the intention of causing such bodily injury as is likely to cause death, or with
-- the knowledge that he is likely by such act to cause death, commits culpable
-- homicide.
--
-- Explanations 1 and 2 are rules of causation, not further elements: accelerating
-- the death of a person already labouring under a disorder is causing it, and so is
-- inflicting an injury from which proper treatment might have saved the victim.
-- Both are encoded as alternative routes to `causes death by doing an act` rather
-- than as conjuncts, because each supplies causation where it might be doubted.
--
-- Explanation 3 is a limit and is encoded as one: causing the death of a child in
-- the mother's womb is not homicide, but it may be culpable homicide to cause the
-- death of a living child if any part has been brought forth, though the child may
-- not have breathed or been completely born. The womb case is therefore excluded
-- unless the bringing-forth fact is asserted. ss 312 to 316 are the sections that
-- deal with the unborn child, and they are not encoded in this module.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 299 Explanations 1 and 2"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the death is caused by the act` IF
        hm's `causes death by doing an act`
    OR  hm's `the deceased was labouring under a disorder disease or bodily infirmity and the bodily injury accelerated the death`
    OR  hm's `the death might have been prevented by resorting to proper remedies and skilful treatment`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 299 Explanation 3"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Explanation 3 excludes the death from homicide` IF
        hm's `the death caused is that of a child in the mother womb`
    AND (NOT hm's `any part of the child had been brought forth though the child may not have breathed or been completely born`)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 299"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes culpable homicide under Penal Code 1871 section 299
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc Death caused by an act, with the intention of causing death, or of causing bodily injury likely to cause death, or with knowledge that death is likely.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes culpable homicide` IF
        `the death is caused by the act` hm
    AND (   hm's `with the intention of causing death`
         OR hm's `with the intention of causing such bodily injury as is likely to cause death`
         OR hm's `with the knowledge that he is likely by such act to cause death`)
    AND (NOT `Explanation 3 excludes the death from homicide` hm)

§§ `Section 300 -- Murder`

-- Except in the cases hereinafter excepted, culpable homicide is murder if the act
-- is done (a) with the intention of causing death; (b) with the intention of causing
-- such bodily injury as the offender knows to be likely to cause the death of the
-- person to whom the harm is caused; (c) with the intention of causing bodily injury
-- sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death; or (d) with knowledge
-- that the act is so imminently dangerous that it must in all probability cause
-- death or such bodily injury as is likely to cause death, and without any excuse
-- for incurring that risk.
--
-- Illustration (b) is the one that separates (b) from (c): striking a man known to
-- be diseased is murder under (b) though the blow would not kill a healthy person;
-- the same blow struck in ignorance of the disease is not.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300(a) to (d)"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 300 murder limb is met` IF
        hm's `the act by which the death is caused is done with the intention of causing death`
    OR  hm's `with the intention of causing such bodily injury as the offender knows to be likely to cause the death of the person to whom the harm is caused`
    OR  hm's `with the intention of causing bodily injury to any person and the bodily injury intended to be inflicted is sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death`
    OR  hm's `knows that the act is so imminently dangerous that it must in all probability cause death or such bodily injury as is likely to cause death and commits it without any excuse for incurring the risk`

§§ `Section 300 Exception 1 -- Grave and sudden provocation`

-- Culpable homicide is not murder if the offender, whilst deprived of the power of
-- self-control by grave and sudden provocation, causes the death of the person who
-- gave the provocation, or of any other person by mistake or accident.
--
-- The three provisos each defeat the Exception: provocation sought or voluntarily
-- provoked as an excuse; provocation the offender knew or had reason to believe was
-- given by something done in obedience to the law or by a public servant lawfully
-- exercising his powers; provocation given by something done in the lawful exercise
-- of the right of private defence. Illustrations (c), (d) and (e) are each a proviso
-- case, and each is murder.
--
-- Explanation 1 makes gravity and suddenness a question of fact judged against an
-- ordinary person of the same gender and age, sharing such characteristics as would
-- affect the gravity of the provocation, placed in the same situation. It is encoded
-- as a conjunct because without it the Exception does not apply -- Illustration (g),
-- the case of prolonged abuse, turns on exactly that test.
-- Explanation 2 provides that provocation may be words, gestures or conduct or any
-- combination. It widens what may be asserted and adds no limb.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exception 1"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Exception 1 applies` IF
        hm's `whilst deprived of the power of self-control by grave and sudden provocation causes the death of the person who gave the provocation or of any other person by mistake or accident`
    AND hm's `an ordinary person of the same gender and age sharing such characteristics as would affect the gravity of the provocation and placed in the same situation would be deprived of self-control by it`
    AND (NOT hm's `the provocation was sought or voluntarily provoked by the offender as an excuse for killing or doing harm to any person`)
    AND (NOT hm's `the offender knew or had reason to believe that the provocation was given by anything done in obedience to the law or by a public servant in the lawful exercise of the powers of such public servant`)
    AND (NOT hm's `the provocation was given by anything done in the lawful exercise of the right of private defence`)

§§ `Section 300 Exception 2 -- Exceeding the right of private defence`

-- Culpable homicide is not murder if the offender, in the exercise of the right of
-- private defence of person or property, exceeds the power given to him by law and
-- causes the death of the person against whom he is exercising that right, without
-- premeditation and without any intention of doing more harm than is necessary for
-- the purpose of the defence.
--
-- Note how this sits against Chapter 4A. If the right is NOT exceeded, s 96 makes
-- the act no offence at all and no question of murder arises. Exception 2 is for the
-- case where Chapter 4A has run out: the right existed, was exceeded, and the most
-- it can now do is reduce murder to culpable homicide. The encoding keeps the two
-- separate -- `a private defence justification applies` is tested by the wrapper,
-- this Exception by the murder rule.
--
-- The Explanation defines "premeditation" as an intention formed before the
-- circumstances that gave rise to the act of private defence, to cause death within
-- s 300(a), or bodily injury within s 300(b) or (c), or to do an act knowing it
-- imminently dangerous within s 300(d). It is carried as the single fact
-- `without premeditation as defined in the Explanation to Exception 2`.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exception 2"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Exception 2 applies` IF
        hm's `in the exercise of the right of private defence of person or property exceeds the power given by law and causes the death of the person against whom the right is exercised`
    AND hm's `without premeditation as defined in the Explanation to Exception 2`
    AND hm's `without any intention of doing more harm than is necessary for the purpose of such defence`

§§ `Section 300 Exceptions 3 to 7`

-- Exception 3: a public servant, or one aiding a public servant acting for the
-- advancement of public justice, who exceeds his powers and causes death by an act
-- he in good faith believes lawful and necessary for the due discharge of his duty,
-- without ill will towards the deceased.
-- Exception 4: culpable homicide committed without premeditation in a sudden fight
-- in the heat of passion upon a sudden quarrel, without the offender having taken
-- undue advantage or acted in a cruel or unusual manner, subject to the proviso that
-- he did not know and had no reason to believe that the deceased was acting in
-- obedience to the law or was a public servant lawfully exercising his powers.
-- Its four Explanations: it is immaterial which party provoked or struck first; a
-- "fight" includes blows that do not land and a single blow; "premeditation" is as
-- in Exception 2; a "quarrel" needs no verbal exchange. Illustration (a) fails for
-- want of a fight; Illustration (b) for undue advantage.
-- Exception 5: the deceased, being above 18, suffers death or takes the risk of
-- death with his own consent.
-- Exception 6: a woman who voluntarily causes the death of her child below 12 months
-- while the balance of her mind was disturbed by the effect of the birth or of
-- lactation consequent on it. This is s 310 infanticide seen from the murder side.
-- Exception 7: abnormality of mind substantially impairing the capacity to know the
-- nature of the acts, or to know whether they are wrong, or the power to control
-- them. The second of those three applies only if the impairment extended to knowing
-- both that the acts are wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest
-- persons AND wrong as contrary to law -- the same two-limb structure as s 84(2).

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exception 3"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Exception 3 applies` IF
        hm's `being a public servant or aiding a public servant acting for the advancement of public justice exceeds the powers given to him by law`
    AND hm's `causes death by doing an act which he in good faith believes to be lawful and necessary for the due discharge of his duty as such public servant`
    AND hm's `without ill will towards the person whose death is caused`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exception 4"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Exception 4 applies` IF
        hm's `committed without premeditation in a sudden fight in the heat of passion upon a sudden quarrel`
    AND hm's `without the offender having taken undue advantage or acted in a cruel or unusual manner`
    AND (NOT hm's `the offender knew or had reason to believe that the person whose death was caused was acting in obedience to the law or was a public servant acting in the lawful exercise of the powers of such public servant`)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exception 5"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Exception 5 applies` IF
    hm's `the person whose death is caused being above 18 years of age suffers death or takes the risk of death with his own consent`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exception 6"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Exception 6 applies` IF
        hm's `the offender being a woman voluntarily causes the death of her child being a child below 12 months of age`
    AND hm's `at the time of the offence the balance of her mind was disturbed by reason of her not having fully recovered from the effect of giving birth to the child or by reason of the effect of lactation consequent upon the birth`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exception 7"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Exception 7(a)(ii) impairment is made out` IF
        hm's `such abnormality of mind substantially impaired his capacity to know whether those acts or omissions are wrong`
    AND hm's `there was a substantial impairment of his capacity to know that the acts or omissions are wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
    AND hm's `there was a substantial impairment of his capacity to know that the acts or omissions are wrong as contrary to law`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exception 7"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Exception 7 applies` IF
        hm's `at the time of the acts or omissions causing the death the offender was suffering from such abnormality of mind as substantially impaired his capacity to know the nature of those acts or omissions`
    OR  `the Exception 7(a)(ii) impairment is made out` hm
    OR  hm's `such abnormality of mind substantially impaired his power to control his acts or omissions`

§§ `Murder, and culpable homicide not amounting to murder`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300 Exceptions 1 to 7"
@export Decide whether any Penal Code 1871 section 300 Exception takes the culpable homicide out of murder
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc The homicide facts. Returns TRUE if any of the seven Exceptions to s 300 is made out.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 300 Exception applies` IF
        `Exception 1 applies` hm
    OR  `Exception 2 applies` hm
    OR  `Exception 3 applies` hm
    OR  `Exception 4 applies` hm
    OR  `Exception 5 applies` hm
    OR  `Exception 6 applies` hm
    OR  `Exception 7 applies` hm

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 300"
@export Decide whether the culpable homicide is murder under Penal Code 1871 section 300
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc Culpable homicide under s 299, one of the four s 300 limbs, and none of the seven Exceptions.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes murder` IF
        `constitutes culpable homicide` hm
    AND `a section 300 murder limb is met` hm
    AND (NOT `a section 300 Exception applies` hm)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 299"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes culpable homicide not amounting to murder under Penal Code 1871
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc Culpable homicide under s 299 which is not murder -- either because no s 300 limb is met, or because an Exception applies.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes culpable homicide not amounting to murder` IF
        `constitutes culpable homicide` hm
    AND (NOT `constitutes murder` hm)

§§ `Section 301 -- Death of a person other than the one intended`

-- If a person, by doing anything which he intends or knows to be likely to cause
-- death, commits culpable homicide by causing the death of any person whose death he
-- neither intends nor knows himself to be likely to cause, the culpable homicide is
-- of the description it would have been had he killed the person intended.
--
-- s 301 transfers the description, not liability itself -- it is the homicide
-- counterpart of s 26G transferred fault. The rule below reports that the transfer
-- is engaged; what it transfers into is decided by `constitutes murder` on the same
-- facts, since the offender's own intention is what the s 300 limbs test.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 301(1)"
@export Decide whether Penal Code 1871 section 301 transfers the description of the culpable homicide to the death actually caused
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc An act intended or known to be likely to cause death, which causes the death of someone whose death was neither intended nor known to be likely.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 301 transfers the description of the culpable homicide` IF
        `constitutes culpable homicide` hm
    AND hm's `by doing anything which he intends or knows to be likely to cause death causes the death of a person whose death he neither intends nor knows himself to be likely to cause`

§§ `Section 304A -- Causing death by a rash or negligent act`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 304A"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes causing death by a rash or negligent act under Penal Code 1871 section 304A
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc Death caused by a rash or negligent act not amounting to culpable homicide.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes causing death by a rash or negligent act` IF
        hm's `causes the death of any person by doing a rash or negligent act not amounting to culpable homicide`
    AND (NOT `constitutes culpable homicide` hm)

§§ `Sections 304B and 304C -- Death of a child, domestic worker or vulnerable person`

-- s 304B: a relevant person who causes the death of a child, domestic worker or
-- vulnerable person by sustained abuse. "Sustained abuse" is defined in s 304B(2) as
-- a course of conduct consisting of voluntarily causing hurt or knowingly causing
-- neglect, or both, on 2 or more occasions, or on a single occasion if protracted.
-- "Relevant person" is the one with custody, charge or care, or the employer of a
-- domestic worker; "neglect" and "vulnerable person" likewise come from s 304B(2)
-- and s 74A(5). Those are carried as caller-asserted status facts.
--
-- s 304C: the same victims, but the offence is causing OR ALLOWING the death within
-- a household. s 304C(1)(d) is a true alternative -- either the accused's own act
-- caused the death, or he was aware of the significant risk, failed to take
-- reasonable protective steps, and the act occurred in circumstances he foresaw or
-- ought to have foreseen. s 304C(2) provides that the prosecution need not prove
-- which alternative applies, which is a rule about proof and adds no limb.
-- s 304C(3) is a defence to the second alternative only: the accused could not have
-- been expected in his circumstances to take any such step. It is encoded as a
-- negated conjunct on that alternative, not on the whole section, because an accused
-- whose own act caused the death cannot be heard to say he could not have protected
-- the victim from himself.
-- s 304C(5) supplies the meaning of "member of a household" and of "unlawful act",
-- which is an act constituting an offence or one that would but for the actor being
-- within s 82 or s 83 or entitled to rely on unsoundness of mind, intoxication or
-- mistake of fact. As in s 335A, that set is one caller-asserted fact: applying the
-- Chapter 4 tests would need the third party's own facts.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 304B"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes causing death by sustained abuse under Penal Code 1871 section 304B
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc A relevant person causing the death of a child below 14, domestic worker or vulnerable person by a course of conduct of voluntarily causing hurt or knowingly causing neglect, on 2 or more occasions or on one protracted occasion.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes causing death by sustained abuse` IF
        hm's `the victim is a child below 14 years of age a domestic worker or a vulnerable person`
    AND hm's `the accused is a relevant person in relation to the victim within section 304B(2)`
    AND hm's `causes the death of the victim by a course of conduct consisting of voluntarily causing hurt or knowingly causing neglect or both`
    AND (   hm's `that course of conduct occurred on 2 or more occasions`
         OR hm's `that course of conduct occurred on a single occasion and was protracted`)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 304C(1)(d) and (3)"
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the section 304C(1)(d) alternative is made out` IF
        hm's `the accused was the person whose act caused the victim death`
    OR  (    hm's `the accused was or ought to have been aware of that significant risk`
         AND hm's `the accused failed to take such steps as could reasonably have been expected to protect the victim from that significant risk`
         AND hm's `the unlawful act occurred in circumstances of the kind that the accused foresaw or ought to have foreseen`
         AND (NOT hm's `the accused could not have been expected in the accused circumstances to take any such step`))

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 304C(1)"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes causing or allowing the death of a child, domestic worker or vulnerable person in the same household under Penal Code 1871 section 304C
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc The victim's death by the unlawful act of a household member with frequent contact, the accused's own membership and contact, a significant risk of grievous hurt, and either the accused's own act or his failure to protect.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes causing or allowing a household death` IF
        hm's `the victim is a child below 14 years of age a domestic worker or a vulnerable person`
    AND hm's `the victim dies as a result of the unlawful act of a person who was a member of the same household as the victim and had frequent contact with the victim`
    AND hm's `the accused was a member of the same household as the victim and had frequent contact with the victim at the time of that act`
    AND hm's `at that time there was a significant risk of grievous hurt being caused to the victim by the unlawful act of such a person`
    AND `the section 304C(1)(d) alternative is made out` hm

§§ `Sections 305 and 306 -- Abetment of suicide`

-- s 306: if any person attempts or commits suicide, whoever abets that attempt or
-- suicide is guilty. s 305: the aggravated form, where the person who attempted or
-- committed suicide was a minor below 18 or a person who lacks capacity to
-- understand the consequences of the attempt, and the abettor knew or ought
-- reasonably to have known it.
-- s 305(1)(a), (b) and (c) differ only in punishment -- suicide completed, attempt,
-- and attempt where hurt is caused to any person. The three facts are carried so a
-- caller can see which case it is; no term is computed.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 306"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes abetment of suicide or attempted suicide under Penal Code 1871 section 306
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc Abetment of a suicide or attempted suicide which in fact occurred.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes abetment of suicide` IF
        hm's `abets the commission of the suicide or attempted suicide`
    AND (   hm's `that person commits suicide`
         OR hm's `that person attempts suicide`)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 305(1)"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes abetment of the suicide of a minor or person who lacks capacity under Penal Code 1871 section 305
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc Abetment of the suicide or attempted suicide of a minor below 18 or a person lacking capacity to understand its consequences, where the abettor knew or ought reasonably to have known that.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes abetment of the suicide of a minor or person lacking capacity` IF
        `constitutes abetment of suicide` hm
    AND hm's `the person who committed or attempted suicide is a minor below 18 years of age or a person who lacks capacity to understand the consequences of attempting or committing suicide`
    AND hm's `knew or ought reasonably to have known that the person was such a minor or person who lacks capacity`

§§ `Sections 307 and 308 -- Attempt to murder, attempt to commit culpable homicide`

-- s 307: any act done with the intention of causing death, in such circumstances
-- that if death were caused the actor would be guilty of murder.
-- s 308: the same, where he would be guilty of culpable homicide not amounting to
-- murder. Illustration to s 308: firing a pistol on grave and sudden provocation.
-- Both are complete without death. Illustration (b) to s 307 -- throwing a child
-- into a river with intent to kill -- is the offence though the child lives.
-- Illustration (c) -- buying and loading a gun intending murder -- is not yet within
-- the section, which is the same substantial-step line s 511 draws.
-- Hurt caused by the act raises the punishment in both sections; the fact is carried
-- and no term is computed.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 307(1)"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes attempt to murder under Penal Code 1871 section 307
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc An act done with the intention of causing death, in circumstances where causing death would be murder. Death need not result.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes attempt to murder` IF
        hm's `does any act with the intention of causing death`
    AND hm's `under such circumstances that if he by that act caused death he would be guilty of murder`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 308"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes attempt to commit culpable homicide under Penal Code 1871 section 308
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc An act done with the intention of causing death, in circumstances where causing death would be culpable homicide not amounting to murder.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes attempt to commit culpable homicide` IF
        hm's `does any act with the intention of causing death`
    AND hm's `under such circumstances that if he by that act caused death he would be guilty of culpable homicide not amounting to murder`

§§ `Section 308A -- Causing death in furtherance of a group's object`

-- Being or acting as a member of a group, knowing that the common object of its
-- members is to commit an offence, and knowing either that death or grievous hurt is
-- likely to be caused in furtherance of that object or that a deadly weapon is to be
-- used against another person in furtherance of it, where the death of a person was
-- in fact caused in furtherance of the common object.
-- The Illustration is the member who joins a robbery believing the leader's knife is
-- only to threaten with. "Group" takes its meaning from s 2(1) of the Organised
-- Crime Act 2015, which is not in this source bundle, so membership is asserted.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 308A(1)"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes causing death in furtherance of a group's object under Penal Code 1871 section 308A
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc Membership of a group with a criminal common object, knowledge that death or grievous hurt is likely or that a deadly weapon is to be used, and a death in fact caused in furtherance of that object.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes causing death in furtherance of a group object` IF
        hm's `is or acts as a member of a group`
    AND hm's `knowing that the common object of the members of the group is to commit an offence under this Code or any written law`
    AND (   hm's `knowing that death or grievous hurt is likely to be caused in furtherance of the group common object`
         OR hm's `knowing that a deadly weapon or anything which used as a weapon of offence is likely to cause death is to be used against another person in furtherance of that common object`)
    AND hm's `the death of a person was caused in furtherance of the group common object`

§§ `Section 308B -- Concealment, desecration or disposal of a corpse`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 308B(1)"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes concealment, desecration or disposal of a corpse under Penal Code 1871 section 308B
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc Intentional or knowing concealment, desecration or disposal of a human corpse which impedes or prevents its discovery or identification, or the detection, investigation or prosecution of an offence.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes concealment or disposal of a corpse` IF
        hm's `intentionally or knowingly conceals desecrates or disposes of a human corpse`
    AND (   hm's `by such act impedes or prevents the discovery or identification of a human corpse`
         OR hm's `by such act impedes or prevents the detection investigation or prosecution of an offence under this Code or any other written law`)

§§ `Section 310 -- Infanticide`

-- A woman who by any intentional act or omission causes the death of her child below
-- 12 months of age, the balance of her mind being disturbed by the effect of the
-- birth or of lactation consequent upon it, is guilty of infanticide notwithstanding
-- that but for this section the offence would have amounted to murder.
--
-- s 310 and Exception 6 to s 300 cover the same ground from opposite directions --
-- Exception 6 takes the case out of murder, s 310 names what it then is. They share
-- the balance-of-mind fact but not the conduct fact: Exception 6 says "voluntarily
-- causes the death", s 310 "by any intentional act or omission causes the death".

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 310"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes infanticide under Penal Code 1871 section 310
GIVEN hm IS A Homicide @desc A woman causing the death of her child below 12 months by an intentional act or omission, while the balance of her mind was disturbed by the effect of the birth or of lactation.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes infanticide` IF
        hm's `the woman by an intentional act or omission causes the death of her child being a child below 12 months of age`
    AND hm's `at the time of the offence the balance of her mind was disturbed by reason of her not having fully recovered from the effect of giving birth to the child or by reason of the effect of lactation consequent upon the birth`

§§ `Proposed Act wrappers`

GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposed act constitutes murder` IF
        `constitutes murder` (a's `homicide`)
    AND NOT (`the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` a)

GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposed act constitutes culpable homicide` IF
        `constitutes culpable homicide` (a's `homicide`)
    AND NOT (`the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` a)

GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposed act constitutes causing death by a rash or negligent act` IF
        `constitutes causing death by a rash or negligent act` (a's `homicide`)
    AND NOT (`the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` a)

GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposed act constitutes attempt to murder` IF
        `constitutes attempt to murder` (a's `homicide`)
    AND NOT (`the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` a)

GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposed act constitutes abetment of suicide` IF
        `constitutes abetment of suicide` (a's `homicide`)
    AND NOT (`the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` a)
